{"id": "5168", "revid": "1812", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=5168", "title": "Burntisland", "text": "Burntisland is a port on the north coast of the Firth of Forth, in Fife in central Scotland, with a population of 6300 in 2022. Its west side is industrial and formerly had ship-building, but its east (downriver) is a small seaside resort and commuter town for Edinburgh. Its name refers to an island in the harbour that was long ago engulfed by shoreline development. The usual story is that huts there caught fire, but it could derive from \"Burnet's land\" a bygone landowner. \nGet in.\nSee Fife for inter-city routes. From Edinburgh Airport take Bus 747 (24 hours) across the river and change to train or bus in Inverkeithing. \nBy train.\nInter-city trains seldom stop in Burntisland so you need to change at Edinburgh Waverley, Haymarket or Inverkeithing. Stopping trains run from Edinburgh (M-Sa twice an hour, Su hourly) via Haymarket (for airport), Inverkeithing (for Dunfermline), Dalgety Bay and Aberdour, taking 40 min. They continue to Kinghorn, Kirkcaldy, then either Leven, or Markinch (for Glenrothes) and Perth, or Leuchars (for St Andrews) and Dundee. \n is near the docks. It has a ticket office staffed M-Sa and ticket machines but no toilets. There is level access to Platform 1 (for Kirkcaldy) and a ramp to Platform 2 (for Edinburgh). \nFrom 1847 to 1890 the world's first ro-ro ferry plied across the Forth between Burntisland and Granton near Edinburgh: railway trucks were shunted on and off the ferry without needing unloading, while passengers walked. It closed once the railway bridge spanned the Forth. Dr Bell of Edinburgh, one of the main inspirations for Sherlock Holmes, once theatrically deduced that a patient had come by this ferry by the nature of the mud on his boots. Much too simple to notice the patient's home address on the case notes and make an obvious inference.\nBy bus.\nInter-city buses don't call here. Stagecoach East Scotland Bus 7 runs along the coast from Dunfermline to Rosyth, Inverkeithing, Dalgety Bay, Aberdour, Burntisland, Kinghorn, Kirkcaldy and Leven, M-Sa every 30 min and hourly on Sunday. The main bus stop is by the junction of High St and Kirkgate.\nBy road.\nFrom the south cross the Forth on M90 Queen's Crossing Bridge (no toll) and take exit 1C onto A921 eastbound.\nGet around.\nThe town can be explored on foot. Bus 7 (above) gets you along the coast to Kinghorn and beyond. \nTaxi firms are Island Cars (+44 1592 874321), Maginns (+44 7384 475884) and Lee's (+44 1592 871234).\nEat.\nSit-down places are along High St: Burger Island, Gooz BBQ House, Cafe Verde and Arina Meze; plus takeaways.\nConnect.\nAs of Sep 2026, Burntisland and its approach roads have 4G from Three and Vodafone, and 5G from EE and O2."}
{"id": "5170", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=5170", "title": "Burqin", "text": "Burqin (\u5e03\u5c14\u6d25; B\u00f9'\u011brj\u012bn) is a city in the northern part of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region, near the Russian border.\nHemu Village and Kanas Scenic area are located in Burqin, and it begins to snow during September-October every year, is a good place for skiing.\nUnderstand.\nBurqin County has jurisdiction over four towns, two townships and one ethnic township: Burqin Town, Chonghuer Town, Woimok Town, Kuastek Town, Dulaiti Township, Yegezitobe Township and Hemuhanas Mongolian Township.\nThe scenic spots are mainly distributed in Burqin County and Hemu Hanas Mongolian Township.\nHistory.\nBurqin was the nomadic land of the Xiongnu in the Western Han Dynasty. The Three Kingdoms belonged to Xianbei, the Sui Dynasty belonged to the Western Turks, the Tang Dynasty belonged to the Beiting capital protection House, and then to the Western Liao. After the Qing Dynasty put down the Dzunggar, the area of Burqin was under the control of Kobudo Counsellors under the left deputy general of Ulya Sutai Dingbian, and it was also a nomadic area under the left wing of Altai Ulianghai. Qing Guangxu thirty years (1905), Kobudo, Altai divided, set up Altai minister, under the control of the Qing Court, Burqin under the jurisdiction of Altai Minister. In the early period of the Republic of China, Altai followed the old system of the Qing Dynasty and was directly under the jurisdiction of the central government. In the three years of the Republic of China (1914), the Burqin River was disintegrated by Chenghua County and was named for being near the Burqin River. In the eighth year of the Republic of China (1919), Altai was assigned to Xinjiang Province and Ashan Road was set up. In the same year, Burqin officially set up a county, has been under the jurisdiction of Ashandao, Ashan Administrative district, Ashan Special District, Altai Special District, 1970 after the Altai region.\nClimate.\nBurqin County, located at the southwest foot of the Altai Mountains in northern Xinjiang and the northern edge of Junggar Basin, belongs to the northern temperate cold climate zone. Its main climatic characteristics are: cold and long winter, hot and dry summer, short spring and autumn, four distinct seasons, sufficient light, scarce precipitation, evaporation. Due to the large difference between the north and the south, the vertical distribution of climate characteristics in the county is obvious.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nBurqin buses cost \u00a51 per person between any two stops within the town. Just put \u00a51 in the container."}
